{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CgboSAPaRwYsyYxZuoh5J","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I rewrote `rbac.yaml` so `log-shipper` gets only what it needs, but the live cluster still has the old `cluster-admin` binding.\n\n**What changed in `rbac.yaml`:**\n- Removed the `ClusterRoleBinding` `log-shipper-admin` that bound `cluster-admin`.\n- Added a namespaced `Role` `log-shipper` in `k8s-rbac-least-privilege` with:\n  - `pods`: `list`, `watch`\n  - `pods/log`: `get`\n- Added a `RoleBinding` `log-shipper` that binds that Role to the `log-shipper` ServiceAccount.\n- The ServiceAccount is unchanged.\n\nA client-side dry run validates all three objects.\n\n**Still open:** `kubectl get` shows the `log-shipper-admin` ClusterRoleBinding is live, so `kubectl auth can-i` will keep returning yes for cluster-wide actions until it is deleted. Re-applying this file does not remove it. I did not delete it because you asked me to work only inside this directory. To finish the fix on the cluster, run `kubectl delete clusterrolebinding log-shipper-admin` and then `kubectl apply -f rbac.yaml`.\n\nI did not apply the manifests to the cluster or check `auth can-i` against the live state, since the verifier re-applies them itself."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":494,"cache_read_input_tokens":21765,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":494},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7b38bcb1-86d1-4366-9d26-3f7b302291f6","uuid":"f125351c-bb8d-4c95-ad34-fc0963a073b8","timestamp":"2026-11-01T07:19:03.651Z","request_id":"req_011CgboS9wnfFFnsoSEVZpYn"}
